  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 256 AM CDT Sat Aug 8 2026\n\nWater vapor imagery this morning shows a fairly strong ridge over \nthe western CONUS, with broad troughing in place towards the Great \nLakes. Broad synoptic subsidence in place across most of the Plains, \nkeeping clear skies and quiet conditions across eastern KS. This \nshould continue through the rest of the day, with temperatures \nwarming into the low 90s. There is an outlier scenario suggested by \nmainly the RRFS/REFS, where thunderstorms develop this morning in \nnorthwest Nebraska and move southeast into northern KS this \nafternoon before dissipating. While a weak shortwave is visible \ntowards this area on water vapor, high CIN with warm mid-level \ntemperatures leads to very low confidence in anything occurring. So \nwill stick with a dry forecast. A similar low probability (15%) scenario\ncould occur overnight, with any storms developing in Nebraska \npotentially briefly moving into KS before weakening. The severe \nthreat with these would be quite low, but some gusty winds (40-60 \nmph) could briefly occur as storms decay.\n\nIf overnight storms do not occur Saturday night, Sunday is shaping \nup to be a warm and humid day. The upper ridge to our west will \nweaken some but flatten out eastward as flow turns more zonal, \nshifting the 850 mb thermal ridge closer to the forecast area. Still \njust enough uncertainty to hold off on a Heat Advisory, but the most \nlikely scenario is for heat indices to climb into the 104 to 108 \ndegree range across much of the area.\n\nMonday through Wednesday, the heat should continue. A seasonably \nstrong zonal jet looks to remain just to our north, with a quasi-\nstationary boundary somewhere to our north as well. Similar to \nSunday, the pattern remains favorable for convection to our north \nthat could result in temperature busts if outflow moves southward \ninto northeast Kansas. But the most likely scenario will continue to \nbe for mostly sunny skies south of the boundary, allowing \ntemperatures to climb into the mid/upper 90s and heat indices in the \nlow/mid 100s.\n\nBy the second half of the week, guidance remains consistent in a \nstronger shortwave diving southeast across the Upper Midwest. Still \nslight differences in the timing and depth of this system, with some \nrecent guidance delaying the arrival by a day or so. Regardless, it \nshould eventually push a cold front southward into the area. \nIncreased shower and storm chances will arrive with and behind the \nfront whenever it does move through.",
